{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31328,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\ntitle: \"Trotterized Hubbard Model\"\ntitle-block-banner: true\nabstract: |\n  Trotter error bound but more relevant for physics\n---","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!---\ntodo: Check neel state set up\nWhat is formular for order bound Trotter\n--->","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Generic resource estimation overestimates Trotter cost for structured Hamiltonians like Hubbard by X. We show this empirically using spectral norm, commutator bounds, and state-specific fidelity on a 2×2 model. We then demonstrate how Hubbard's bipartite and commutative structure can be exploited to reduce the resource estimate by factor Y","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-31T11:55:56.699386Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-31T11:55:56.694791Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-31T11:55:56.744718Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-31T11:55:56.74089Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-31T11:55:56.699244Z"}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Motivation for Trotterization\nWhy QSVT with QROM vs Trooterization\n\nHubbard is local and structured\nQSVT/QROM is especially powerful when the Hamiltonian is given as a large irregular table: But the Hubbard model on a regular lattice is not an arbitrary table. It has simple repeated structure:\n\n- nearest-neighbor hopping,\n- osite interaction,\n- mostly uniform coefficients,\n- sparse local geometry.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# | code-fold: true\n# | code-summary: \"Import libraries\"\nimport logging\n\nfrom scipy import sparse\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\nimport pennylane as qp\nimport pennylane.estimator as qre\nfrom pennylane.resource import SpectralNormError\nimport time as pytime\nfrom tqdm import tqdm\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:11:37.001341Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:11:37.001699Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:11:37.007196Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:11:37.001666Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:11:37.006189Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":32},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Setup {#sec-setup}","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We create a $2 \\times 2$ rectangle, with 4 sites, and 8 qubits. The notion is\n\n\n| Qubit | Spin orbital |\n|-------|--------------|\n| 0     | site 0 ↑     |\n| 1     | site 0 ↓     |\n| 2     | site 1 ↑     |\n| 3     | site 1 ↓     |\n| 4     | site 2 ↑     |\n| 5     | site 2 ↓     |\n| 6     | site 3 ↑     |\n| 7     | site 3 ↓     |\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"t = 1.0\nU = 4.0\ntime = 3  # The time of evolution (t in exp(-iHt))\n\n# number of Trotter steps, proposally not good to show the power of higher Trotter order\nn_steps = int(time * 4)\nnum_steps = [10, 20, 40, 80, 160]\n\nn_cells = [2, 2, 1]\norders = [1, 2, 4, 6]\nH = qp.spin.fermi_hubbard(\n    lattice=\"cubic\",\n    n_cells=n_cells,\n    hopping=t,\n    coulomb=U,\n    boundary_condition=False,\n    mapping=\"jordan_wigner\",\n)\nn_qubits = H.num_wires","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:20:29.406449Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:20:29.406821Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:20:29.421462Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:20:29.406774Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:20:29.420417Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":43},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"This link is interesting https://fermi-hubbard-commutators.readthedocs.io/en/latest/commutator_bounds.html","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Naive Trotter error bound\n\nHow badly do standard Trotter error bounds overestimate cost for specific observables in Hubbard, and what's the practical implication for resource estimates?\"\nThe spectral norm $\\lVert {U_{exact} - U_{trotter}}\\rVert_2$ measures the maximum possible deviation over all possible input states in the entire Hilbert space.\n\nHowever in common application, these relevant states (e.g, low-energy excited states with specific symmetries) is in a much smaller space. Therefore it is not the best interest to tune the Trotter using the Spectral Norm.\n\nThe next natural question is for the Hubbard model, the most relavant states are? In this example we use half-filled Neel state ($\\ket{0101...01}$), which is standard for studying Mott insulators.\n\nHaar-random states are common in complexity proofs because they are uniformly distributed across the full Hilbert space, making them worst-case inputs. However, they have no physical relevance to Hubbard physics.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Compute grouping here  https://docs.pennylane.ai/en/stable/code/api/pennylane.ops.op_math.LinearCombination.html#pennylane.ops.op_math.LinearCombination.compute_grouping","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# H.compute_grouping()  # compute the qubit-wise commuting groups!\n\n# resources_exec = qre.estimate(executable_circuit)(grouped_hamiltonian, num_steps, order)\n\n# resources_with_grouping = qre.estimate(\n#     qre.TrotterPauli(kitaev_H_with_grouping, num_steps, order)\n# )\n\n# res = qre.estimate(circuit)(kitaev_H_with_grouping, num_steps, order)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.754589Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.754921Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.766502Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.75489Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.765608Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":21},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Spectral Norm {#secsec-spectral-norm}\n\nExact calculation, with such a small system, the Hamiltonian matrix is tractable ad diagonalizable. It stuggles at 16 qubits, which would result in $2^{16} \\times 2^{16}$ matrix. However, due to the locality nature of Hubbard model, we can save a lot of memory using the sparse matrix","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<!--\n```\nexact_op = qp.exp(H, -1j * time)\nsparse_mat_exact_op = exact_op.sparse_matrix()\nspectral_error = {}\n\nfor order in tqdm.tqdm(orders):\n    approx_op = qp.TrotterProduct(H, n=n_steps, time=time, order=order)\n    sparse_mat_approx_op = sparse.csr_matrix(qp.matrix(approx_op))\n    spectral_error[order] =  sparse.linalg.svds(sparse_mat_exact_op - sparse_mat_approx_op, k=1, which='LM', return_singular_vectors=False)[0]\n\nprint(\n    \"\\n\".join(\n        [f\"Order: {k}, Spectral error: {v:5f}\" for k, v in spectral_error.items()]\n    )\n)```\n-->","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"exact_op = qp.exp(H, -1j * time)\nspectral_error = {}\n\nfor order in orders:\n    approx_op = qp.TrotterProduct(H, n=num_steps[0], time=time, order=order)\n    error = SpectralNormError.get_error(exact_op, approx_op)\n    spectral_error[order] = error\n\nprint(\n    \"\\n\".join(\n        [f\"Order: {k}, Spectral error: {v:5f}\" for k, v in spectral_error.items()]\n    )\n)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.778738Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.77968Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:03:28.279487Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:03:15.779632Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:03:28.278686Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Order: 1, Spectral error: 1.997254\nOrder: 2, Spectral error: 1.998645\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":22},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Childs Method\n\nSince Calculating spectral norm is expensive because it requires diagonalizing the operators. This method promises better memory scalability, but it comes with a cost","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for order in orders[:2]:\n    op = qp.TrotterProduct(H, n=num_steps[0], time=time, order=order)\n    start = pytime.time()\n    one_norm_error_bound = op.error(method=\"one-norm-bound\")\n    commutator_error_bound = op.error(method=\"commutator-bound\")\n    print(f\"Order {order}, Calculation time: {pytime.time() - start}\")\n    print(f\"One-norm bound: {one_norm_error_bound}\")\n    print(f\"Commutator bound: {commutator_error_bound}\")\n    print()","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:03:28.294073Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:03:28.294395Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.094854Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:03:28.294364Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.093928Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Order 1, Calculation time: 1.3183677196502686\nOne-norm bound: SpectralNormError(432.0)\nCommutator bound: SpectralNormError(108.0)\nOrder 2, Calculation time: 145.4651918411255\nOne-norm bound: SpectralNormError(3888.0)\nCommutator bound: SpectralNormError(248.0)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"With this tiny config, it costs a lot of time to compute, and the bound does not seem anywhere positive. They even contradicts each other. Suppose we want to estimate the order of the Trotter operation to get a reliable error, this really put us between a rock and a hard place,\n\nFor an explanation of the contradictions of the methods as well as the time cost, I invite readers to session @secsec-a1\n\nSo we spent a lot of time to calculate these bounds, but end up not gaining anything very informative about how to set the parameters. In larger system, the calculation of SpectralNorm error we did in @secsec-spectral-norm is imposible. \n\nPhysicists only care for limited interesting states, and they live in a small manifold in the Hilbert space. Here we propose using state-aware error estimation","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Physics state-awared error bound estimation\n\n- What are the super set of Neel state? I choose half filled state\n- How does state-dependent error suppression affect the optimal Trotter order?\n    - Perhaps with this we see 1-order is already very good\n    - 2nd order meh\n    - 4th order unncesscary\n    - In the end \"Do physically relevant states reduce the practical advantage of higher-order Trotter formulas in Hubbard simulation?\"","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## State initialization\n\nNeel state ($\\ket{0101...01}$) is the standard state for studying ferromagnetics problem. Using the notation in @sec-setup, we deduce that","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def prepare_neel_state(wires):\n    \"\"\"Néel state: alternating up/down on bipartite lattice\"\"\"\n    for site in range(wires // 2):\n        if site % 2 == 0:\n            qp.PauliX(wires=2 * site)\n        else:\n            qp.PauliX(wires=2 * site + 1)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.096644Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.096966Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.102166Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.096937Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.101256Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":25},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Hubbard Hamiltonian properties\n\nWe have two types of interaction in Hubbard: Hopping $T$ and on-site repulsion $U$","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"dev = qp.device(\"default.qubit\")\n\n\n@qp.qnode(dev)\ndef exact_circ(H, t, wires):\n    \"\"\"\n    Simluate exact evolution\n    \"\"\"\n    prepare_neel_state(wires)\n    qp.exp(-1j * t * H)\n    return qp.state()\n\n\n@qp.qnode(dev)\ndef trotter_circ(H, num_steps, t, wires, order=1):\n    prepare_neel_state(wires)\n    qp.TrotterProduct(H, n=num_steps, time=t, order=order)\n    return qp.state()","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.103218Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.103519Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.121881Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.10348Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.12093Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":26},{"cell_type":"code","source":"resource_est = {}\nfor order in orders:\n    resources_exec = qre.estimate(trotter_circ)(H, n_steps, time, n_qubits, order=order)\n    resource_est[order] = resources_exec.gate_counts","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.123164Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.123537Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.266293Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.123494Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:05:55.265337Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":27},{"cell_type":"code","source":"exact_state = exact_circ(H, time, n_qubits)\nerror_dicts = {}\nstate_fidelities = {}\n\nfor i, n_step in enumerate(tqdm([10, 20, 40, 80, 160])):\n    trotter_state = trotter_circ(H, n_step, time, n_qubits, order=1)\n    state_fidelities[i] = qp.math.fidelity_statevector(exact_state, trotter_state)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:12:38.295673Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:12:38.296039Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:13:02.806052Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:12:38.296009Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:13:02.803322Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"100%|██████████| 5/5 [00:24<00:00,  4.88s/it]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":34},{"cell_type":"code","source":"state_fidelities","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:13:42.662328Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:13:42.662663Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:13:42.668544Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:13:42.662633Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:13:42.66762Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":37,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"{0: np.float64(0.31131315055875886),\n 1: np.float64(0.3367092922531419),\n 2: np.float64(0.33117463143624215),\n 3: np.float64(0.3259996298411703),\n 4: np.float64(0.32295804042156523)}"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":37},{"cell_type":"code","source":"state_fidelities.values()","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:13:26.904375Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:13:26.904765Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:13:26.911312Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:13:26.904733Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:13:26.910467Z"},"trusted":true},"outputs":[{"execution_count":36,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"dict_values([np.float64(0.31131315055875886), np.float64(0.3367092922531419), np.float64(0.33117463143624215), np.float64(0.3259996298411703), np.float64(0.32295804042156523)])"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":36},{"cell_type":"code","source":"error_array = [spectral_error[order] for order in orders]\ngates = [resource_est[order][\"T\"] for order in orders]\n\nplt.plot(gates, error_array, label=\"Spectral Error\")\nfor i, xi, yi in enumerate(zip(gates, error_array)):\n    plt.annotate(\n        f\"Order = {orders[i]}\",\n        (xi, yi),\n        textcoords=\"offset points\",\n        xytext=(0, 8),  # offset label above point\n        ha='center'\n    )\n\nplt.ylabel(\"Error\")\nplt.xlabel(\"T gates\")\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T15:17:30.504067Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T15:17:30.504427Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T15:17:30.655622Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T15:17:30.504394Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T15:17