lt.xlabel(\"Num steps\")\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T14:57:31.105583Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T14:57:31.106968Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T14:57:31.25478Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T14:57:31.106928Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T14:57:31.253872Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":47},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"So we need to have `{python} gates[-1]` T gates to make a simulation for `{python} time` seconds on a modest-sized Hubbard simulation.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Business output","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"If we increase the order based on the Spectral norm estimation, then the number of gates vs accuracy looks like this\n\nBut if we increase the order based on the state fidelity, then the number of gates vs accuracy looks like this\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Goal\n- Practical resource estimation for early-to-intermediate fault-tolerant quantum simulation of strongly correlated materials. Choose 3D Hubbard model using PennyLane's Labs estimator\n- Give systematic, empirical comparison of worst-case vs. observable-specific Trotter error bounds\n- Able to distinguish asymptotic theory from realistic hardware constraints;\n- Capable of producing technically honest estimates that are useful for strategic decision-making inside an FTQC organization.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Appendix","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Appendix 1 {#secsec-a1}\n<!--\nThis seems counter intuitive when commutator bound is more pessimistic than one-norm bound, and none of them are close to the spectral norm. higher order's error should scale with $O(\\frac{t}{n})$. Order 2 is better algorithmically but has worse worst-case combinatorial bound.\n\nOne-norm bound is faster because it just adds every possible error\n\nCommutator bound is tighter here it finds every pair $i, j$ such that [Hᵢ, Hⱼ] = 0. For the Hubbard model, all the interaction terms V = Σ U nᵢ↑nᵢ↓ commute with each other, so this pruning is substantial.\nWhy it runs out of memory at high orders — the combinatorial explosion:\nFor an order-p Trotter formula, the commutator bound requires computing nested commutators to depth p+1. With N Hamiltonian terms:\nTrotter orderCommutator depth needed# evaluations1depth 2~N²2depth 3~N³4depth 5~N⁵-->","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-02T13:07:02.612489Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-02T13:07:02.612885Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-02T13:07:02.617864Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-02T13:07:02.612852Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-02T13:07:02.617044Z"}}}]}